  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Requirements**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving into the user request. I\'m focusing on the core functionality: drag-and-drop seating for students and the repositioning of classroom elements like doors and desks. This seems to be geared towards user interface design and how to visualize and manage classroom layouts. I need to figure out what data structure this interface would need to support these operations, and the best way to represent the classroom elements for interaction.\n\n\n**Verifying the Functionality**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the core functionality: verifying the drag-and-drop mechanism. It\'s crucial that teachers can easily swap student positions within the classroom layout. Specifically, I\'m thinking about the user experience within the \"S\341\272\257p x\341\272\277p S\306\241 \304\221\341\273\223\" (Layout & Seating Editor Mode).  The goal is a seamless interaction, with students being easily dragged from one seat to another, either swapping or filling empty slots.\n\n\n**Defining Interactions**\n\nI\'m currently detailing the specific interactions. The goal is easy student reassignment \342\200\223 moving them to empty seats with an intuitive drag-and-drop. I\'m considering HTML5\'s Drag and Drop API, and touch/click-to-swap fallbacks. Reconfiguring the layout itself involves clicking and dragging architectural slots. I\'m focusing on UI elements: door, teacher desk, podium, student seats, empty space, and aisles. My quick tools palette includes swap and type conversion options.\n\n\n**Implementing New Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating new features. I am prioritizing \"T\341\273\261 \304\221\341\273\231ng x\341\272\277p theo T\341\273\225\" (Auto-arrange by group, seating four groups in rows), \"Kh\303\264i ph\341\273\245c m\341\272\267c \304\221\341\273\213nh\" (Reset to default 42-student layout), and \"L\306\260u s\306\241 \304\221\341\273\223 m\341\273\233i\" (Save layout to local storage/app state with a toast notification). I\'m also incorporating the specified visual assets and crafting the \"Cadre Badges\" component, complete with glowing borders, titles, and appropriate displays throughout the interface. I am also working with emojis.\n\n\n"
