    thinking: "**Re-evaluating Classroom Layout**\n\nI\'m currently considering a classroom redesign. The main idea revolves around swapping the teacher\'s desk with the front door\'s position. I\'m also focused on simplifying the elements, reducing the layout to just a door, a board, and a teacher\'s desk, all in basic rectangular shapes.\n\n\n**Refining the Spatial Arrangement**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the new spatial arrangement. Specifically, I\'m swapping the teacher\'s desk and the front door\'s positions. This is a significant shift. My focus is on reducing the elements to essential rectangular forms for the door, the board, and the teacher\'s desk, while removing any unnecessary grid elements.\n\n\n**Simplifying Visual Indicators**\n\nI\'m now striving for a cleaner aesthetic. I\'ve decided to replace the heavy, boxed cells with plain text labels across the top bar to indicate key elements. The focus is now solely on unboxed text indicators for \"B\303\240n GV,\" \"B\341\272\242NG L\341\273\232P,\" and \"C\341\273\255a ch\303\255nh.\" This minimalist approach extends to the code, specifically updating `SeatGrid.tsx`, `ClassroomLayoutEditor.tsx`, and `mockData.ts` to reflect the clean text format at the classroom\'s front.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Structural Shifts**\n\nI\'ve made a significant structural adjustment. The teacher\'s desk and front door have been swapped, placing the \"B\303\240n GV\" on the left and the \"C\341\273\255a ch\303\255nh\" on the right. Now, I\'m focusing on rendering these elements as clean, unboxed text indicators at the top, specifically \"B\303\240n GV,\" \"[ B\341\272\242NG L\341\273\232P ],\" and \"C\341\273\255a ch\303\255nh.\" The changes are being implemented in `SeatGrid.tsx` to reflect this new architectural header.\n\n\n"
