    thinking: "**Analyzing SeatGrid.tsx**\n\nI\'ve been examining `SeatGrid.tsx` and realized row 0 and row 8, at the top and bottom of the classroom, respectively, were rendering 8 individual seat components. This seems inefficient. The user\'s request for \"V\341\273\213 tr\303\255 b\303\240n gi\303\241o vi\303\252n \304\221\341\273\225i v\341\273\213 tr\303\255\" suggests a direct issue with teacher desk positioning, which relates to the seat component array.\n\n\n**Refining Classroom Layout**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a cleaner classroom layout. I\'ve decided to represent the front and back of the classroom with simple text labels rather than rectangular boxes for the teacher\'s desk, blackboard, and doors. The logic for swapping the teacher\'s desk and front door is solid. I\'m focusing on updating `src/data/mockData.ts` to reflect the new text-based representation, and specifically handling the top row\'s rendering in `SeatGrid.tsx`. I think a text label is much cleaner.\n\n\n**Simplifying Top Bar**\n\nI\'ve made significant progress by replacing the initial clunky rectangular boxes at the top of the classroom with a clean, unified text bar. It presents \"B\303\240n GV,\" \"B\341\272\242NG L\341\273\232P,\" and \"C\341\273\255a ch\303\255nh\" as text labels, enhancing the visual appeal and streamlining the presentation. This is a considerable improvement over the previous approach and makes the top of the classroom uncluttered. I\'m aiming for similar improvements at the bottom.\n\n\n"
